title Six processes where one would do
prompt Keaton: kg.
rota lap 4144, row 4 by the modulus -- Earth, which this seat had already read today at 20260907.002044, so the rota advanced by hand to row 0. Aether, the row that hears: why the work exists, and its pair sound and silence -- sound is the work speaking, silence is the work holding.
think The previous lap left width_check at 75s as the slowest guard and one question to ask of it: what does the loop pay before it skips. The honest answer was nothing -- this scan already filters dated testimony and vendor rooms with a grep BEFORE the loop, so the fault I came looking for is not here.
think That is the Aether reading rather than a dead end. A lap that arrives with a hypothesis and hears the work say no has been told something; the cost was somewhere else, and looking for it is the same act as listening.
obs THE COST WAS SIX PROCESSES PER FILE WHERE ONE ANSWERS. count_authored ran a six-stage grep pipeline -- match usize, drop @intCast, drop @as(usize, drop comment lines, drop extern fn, count whole words -- and it is called once for each of 1,985 files. 11,910 processes to answer a question one awk pass answers, and the pipeline says nothing the single pass cannot say.
obs PROVEN EQUAL BEFORE IT WAS SWAPPED, WHICH IS THE ONLY WAY TO MAKE A GUARD FASTER. I ran both forms over all 1,940 discovered files and compared count for count: ZERO disagreements. Then the whole scan output, old against new, diffed byte-identical. A guard that runs faster and answers differently is not the same guard, and the counts are the only place that could hide.
obs 36.4s AND 40.1s BEFORE, 8.4s AND 8.7s AFTER -- a 78% cut on the counting alone, and the rostered guard fell 75s to 17s end to end. The control still proves 19 checks with zero failures, so the refusals it plants are refused exactly as before.
obs TWO GUARDS NOW, AND THE SHAPE IS ONE SHAPE. Yesterday declared_ceiling paid a fork before a skip; today width_check paid five extra processes for a single count. Both are the same habit -- reaching for a subprocess where the language already has the answer -- and both were invisible because each one costs microseconds and only the multiplication is expensive.
loom width_check calls=1985 processes_before=11910 processes_after=1985
loom width_check counting_s=36.4,40.1_before 8.4,8.7_after cut=78pct agreements=1940_of_1940 disagreements=0
loom width_check guard_wall_s=75_before 17_after control_checks=19 control_failures=0 output=byte_identical
file tools/fixtures/w/width_check_scan.sh count_authored is one awk pass, with every rule of the elder pipeline carried over line for line
scope one Aether lap -- the second-slowest guard made 4x faster, proven equal on every file before the swap
status GREEN -- width-check green, control 19 of 19, scan output diffed byte-identical against the elder form
recommend keep-going -- crushed_index at 59s and borrowed_number at 52s are next, and the question that fits both is now the sharper one: how many processes does this pay per file, and does the language already hold the answer.
